#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# hypergeometric_pfq_regularized

#% text
# `hypergeometric_pfq_regularized(as, bs, z)` gives `hypergeometric_pfq(as, bs, z)` divided by the product of `gamma(b)` over the lower parameters `bs`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The regularized function stays finite when a lower parameter is a non-positive integer, where `hypergeometric_pfq` itself has a pole (DLMF 16.2.5 with the gamma factors).

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq_regularized([1], [2], 1.0)
#% output
#= 1.7182818284590453

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq_regularized([1, 1], [3], 0.5)
#% output
#= 0.6137056388801094

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq_regularized([0.5], [1.5, 2.5], 2.0)
#% output
#= 1.1183952615216628

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The relation to the unregularized function:

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq_regularized([0.5], [2.5], 1.0)*gamma(2.5) - hypergeometric_pfq([0.5], [2.5], 1.0)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A lower parameter at a pole of the unregularized function, where the regularized value is finite, is not evaluated:

#% cell
hypergeometric_pfq_regularized([1], [0], 1.0)
#% output
#= 2.7182818284590455

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `hypergeometric_pfq`, `gamma`, `hypergeometric_1f1`
